# Persistent Static ARP Configuration

This guide explains how I configured a persistent static ARP entry on my system to secure it against ARP spoofing attacks. 
The configuration ensures the system always recognizes the correct MAC address for the gateway.

---

## Steps

### 1. Configure `sudo` Permissions

To allow necessary commands to run without password prompts, I edited the `sudo` configuration. 
This step was essential because some commands require root privileges, and 
I wanted the system to execute them automatically without needing to enter the password every time.

1. Open the `sudo` file:
    
    ```bash
    sudo visudo
    
     Add the following lines at the end of the file:
    
     check ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/arp
     check ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /sbin/ip link set dev eth0 up
     check ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /sbin/ip link set dev eth0 down
    
     Save and exit.
    
    ```
    
2. Create the Static ARP Script

I created a script to automatically add the static ARP entry whenever the network interface comes online. 
This was done to ensure the ARP entry is consistently added each time the network interface is brought up. 
The static ARP entry is crucial for preventing ARP spoofing attacks, where an attacker could impersonate the gateway and redirect traffic.

```
Open the script file for editing:

```

sudo nano /usr/local/bin/set_static_arp.sh

Add the following code:

#!/bin/bash

# Wait until eth0 is up

while ! ip link show eth0 | grep 'state UP' > /dev/null; do
echo "Waiting for eth0 to come up..."
sleep 5
done

# Add static ARP entry

/sbin/arp -s 193.191.176.254 ca:fe:c0:ff:ee:00

Save the file and make it executable:

```
sudo chmod +x /usr/local/bin/set_static_arp.sh

```

1. Automate Script Execution with networkd-dispatcher

To run the script automatically whenever the network comes online, I used networkd-dispatcher. 
This was necessary because I wanted the static ARP entry to be added every time the network interface eth0 is brought up, 
ensuring the correct mapping between IP and MAC addresses without requiring manual intervention.

```
Install networkd-dispatcher if it’s not already installed:

```

sudo apt update
sudo apt install networkd-dispatcher

Navigate to the routable.d directory:

cd /etc/networkd-dispatcher/routable.d/

Create a new script file:

sudo nano [10-arp-fix.sh](http://10-arp-fix.sh/)

Add the following line to execute the ARP script:

#!/bin/bash
/usr/local/bin/set_static_arp.sh

Save the file and make it executable:

```
sudo chmod +x /etc/networkd-dispatcher/routable.d/10-arp-fix.sh

```

1. Testing and Verification

I tested the setup to ensure it works as expected. This was important to confirm that the static ARP 
entry is successfully added when the network interface is up and remains persistent after reboot.
Manually Test the ARP Entry

```
Add the ARP entry manually:

```

sudo arp -s 193.191.176.254 ca:fe:c0:ff:ee:00

Verify it appears in the ARP table:

```
arp -n | grep 193.191.176.254

```

Test the Script

```
Bring the eth0 interface down and up:

```

sudo ip link set dev eth0 down
sudo ip link set dev eth0 up

Verify that the static ARP entry has been added automatically by checking the ARP table:

```
arp -n | grep 193.191.176.254

```

Reboot Test

```
Reboot the system:

```

sudo reboot

Once the system is back online, check the ARP table again:

arp -n | grep 193.191.176.254
